Issue :
## GitHub Immutable OIDC Subject Format Causes AWS Role Assumption Failure

GitHub's newer immutable OIDC subject format can cause AWS IAM trust policies using the traditional repository format to fail with:

```text
Could not assume role with OIDC:
Not authorized to perform sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity
```

Previously, an AWS trust policy might expect:

```text
repo:<OWNER>/<REPOSITORY>:*
```

However, the actual OIDC token can now contain an immutable subject such as:

```text
repo:<OWNER>@<OWNER_ID>/<REPOSITORY>@<REPOSITORY_ID>:environment:dev
```

The important difference is:

```text
Old:
owner/repository

New immutable format:
owner@OWNER_ID/repository@REPOSITORY_ID
```

Because of this, a trust policy configured as:

```json
"token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub": "repo:<OWNER>/<REPOSITORY>:*"
```

does not match the immutable `sub` claim.

The AWS trust policy instead needs to account for the immutable identity:

```json
"token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub":
  "repo:<OWNER>@<OWNER_ID>/<REPOSITORY>@<REPOSITORY_ID>:*"
```

This can particularly affect Terraform configurations that generate the trust condition using:

```hcl
"repo:${var.github_repository}:*"
```

where `github_repository` contains only:

```text
owner/repository
```
*************************************************************************************************************************************************

Solution:

## Solution / Workaround

First, inspect the actual `sub` claim being generated by GitHub's OIDC token. For example:

```yaml
- name: Check OIDC subject
  uses: actions/github-script@v7
  with:
    script: |
      const token = await core.getIDToken('sts.amazonaws.com');
      const payload = JSON.parse(
        Buffer.from(token.split('.')[1], 'base64').toString()
      );

      console.log("aud:", payload.aud);
      console.log("sub:", payload.sub);
```

For repositories using immutable OIDC subjects, the value can have this format:

```text
repo:<OWNER>@<OWNER_ID>/<REPOSITORY>@<REPOSITORY_ID>:environment:<ENVIRONMENT>
```

Update the AWS IAM role trust policy so that its `sub` condition matches the immutable repository identity.

For example, change:

```json
"StringLike": {
  "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub":
    "repo:<OWNER>/<REPOSITORY>:*"
}
```

to:

```json
"StringLike": {
  "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub":
    "repo:<OWNER>@<OWNER_ID>/<REPOSITORY>@<REPOSITORY_ID>:*"
}
```

If the trust policy is managed through Terraform, update the Terraform configuration as well rather than only modifying the IAM role manually.

For example:

```hcl
variable "github_oidc_repository" {
  description = "GitHub immutable OIDC repository identity"
  type        = string
}

# Example value:
# <OWNER>@<OWNER_ID>/<REPOSITORY>@<REPOSITORY_ID>

StringLike = {
  "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub" =
    "repo:${var.github_oidc_repository}:*"
}
```

After updating the trust policy, the GitHub OIDC `sub` and AWS trust condition match, allowing `sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity` to succeed.

For production usage, the condition can be further restricted to specific GitHub environments instead of using `:*`, for example:

```text
repo:<OWNER>@<OWNER_ID>/<REPOSITORY>@<REPOSITORY_ID>:environment:prod
```
